Analogy is a useful way to learn: you map something new onto something you already know, and the new thing becomes less foreign. While learning Go, I found that mapping it onto frontend knowledge made the language's quirks much easier to absorb.

## Syntax side by side

### Importing packages

Go's package imports feel similar to frontend module imports — both use `import` — but Go is leaner: you name the package, not a full path in the same way.

```plain text
// js
import a from "a";
import b from "b"

// go
import (
    "a"
    "b"
)

```

### Variable declarations

```plain text
// js
var a = "a";
let b = "b";
const c = "c";

// go
var x string = "x";
x := "x" // type, declaration, and assignment in one go
```

### Conditionals

```plain text
// js
if (a === "js") {

} else {

}

// go
if a == "go" {

} else {

}
```

### Loops

```plain text
// js
for(let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {

}

// go
for i := 0; i < 100; i++ {

}
```

### Functions

```plain text
// js
function helloJS() {
    console.log("hello js");

    learnJS();
}

function learnJS() {
    console.log("learn js");
}

// go
func helloGo() {
    fmt.Println("hello go");
}

func learnGo() {
    fmt.Println("learn go");

    learnGo();
}
```

Those surface similarities made Go feel approachable — enough that I could start building with it.

